# Plotting data types: automatic_plotting

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

ggplot2's reference page for
[`automatic_plotting`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/automatic_plotting.html) has no
examples, so this notebook says only what the page describes and where Giggly stands.

The page shows how a package can make its own class of data easy to plot with three S3 generics,
each a step further than the last: a `fortify()` method reshapes the data into a long data frame
ggplot2 can use, an `autolayer()` method makes a layer that draws it, and an `autoplot()` method
makes a complete plot, here from that layer. It works through a `my_heatmap` class wrapping a
matrix, drawn with `geom_raster()`. Not in Giggly yet: `fortify()`, `autolayer()` and
`autoplot()`, and `geom_raster()`.
